= Solution
{parent=i}

Define <outgoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates> by $u=t-r_*$. The radial <Schwarzschild metric> becomes
$$
ds^2=-f\,du^2-2\,du\,dr.
$$
Outgoing radio rays have constant $u$. Two nearby wave crests emitted at Alice's <proper times> $\tau$ and $\tau+d\tau$ have separation $du=\dot u_A\,d\tau$. At Bob's fixed radius $r_B$, $dt_B=du$ and $d\tau_B=\sqrt{f_B}\,du$, where $f_B=1-2M/r_B$. Thus the exact local <redshift> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\lambda_B}{\lambda_A}=\frac{d\tau_B}{d\tau_A}=\sqrt{f_B}\,\frac{du_A}{d\tau_A}.}
$$
Here the <wavelength> ratio equals the <proper-time> period ratio in <geometric optics>. Equivalently, the phase gradient $k_a=-\omega_\infty(du)_a$ gives Alice's measured <frequency> $\omega_A=-k_aU_A^a=\omega_\infty\dot u_A$ and Bob's $\omega_B=\omega_\infty/\sqrt{f_B}$.

**In the asymptotically distant-observer limit $f_B\to1$, this becomes $\lambda_B/\lambda_A=du_A/d\tau_A$.** “Far away” is the approximation behind the printed formula; at finite $r_B$ the factor $\sqrt{f_B}$ remains.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For radial free fall, let $E=f\dot t_A>0$ be Alice's conserved <Killing energy>. The inward branch of the <timelike geodesic> has $\dot r=-\sqrt{E^2-f}$, hence
$$
\dot u_A=\dot t_A-\frac{\dot r}{f}=\frac{E+\sqrt{E^2-f}}{f}\sim\frac{2E}{f}.
$$
With $\delta=r-2M$, the near-horizon <geodesic equations> give
$$
f\sim\frac{\delta}{2M},\quad \delta\sim E(\tau_H-\tau),\quad t_A=-2M\log(\tau_H-\tau)+O(1),\quad r_* =2M\log(\tau_H-\tau)+O(1).
$$
The constants in the logarithms are understood to make their arguments dimensionless. Therefore $u_A=t_A-r_*=2t_A+O(1)$. For an outgoing signal reaching the fixed-radius Bob, $t_B=u_A+r_*(r_B)$, so $t_B=2t_A+O(1)$. The <redshift> consequently behaves as
$$
\frac{\lambda_B}{\lambda_A}\propto e^{t_B/(4M)},\qquad \boxed{M=\frac1{4a}}.
$$
This is the answer when $t$ is Bob's <Schwarzschild time>, as appropriate to the stated observation. It is the <Schwarzschild surface gravity> $a=\kappa=1/(4M)$. If $t$ instead means the emission <Schwarzschild time> $t_A$, the same <redshift> is proportional to $e^{t_A/(2M)}$ and gives $M=1/(2a)$. The two answers use different clocks, not different dynamics. If the measured exponential uses Bob's <proper time> $\tau_B$, its rate is $1/(4M\sqrt{f_B})$. In SI units the reception-time result is $M=c^3/(4Ga)$.

The reception-time exponent also holds for smooth radial infall crossing the future <Schwarzschild event horizon> with finite nonzero $dr/d\tau$: regular <ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates> give finite $v$ there, $u=v-2r_*\sim-4M\log(\tau_H-\tau)$. Thus no special value of $E$ is needed.

