= Solution
{parent=i}

The <dominant energy condition> says that, for every future <timelike vector> $u^a$, the energy current $J^a=-T^a{}_b u^b$ is future causal or zero. Choose <normal coordinates> whose future unit time vector at $p$ is $u^a=(1,0,0,0)$. With <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$ and symmetric <stress-energy tensor>,
$$
J^0=T_{00},\qquad J^i=-T_{i0}.
$$
A vector with these components is future causal or zero exactly when
$$
\boxed{T_{00}\ge\sqrt{\sum_iT_{0i}^2}.}
$$
This proves necessity. Conversely, every future unit <timelike vector> can be made the time axis of an orthonormal frame by a proper orthochronous <Lorentz transformation>, and that frame can be extended to <normal coordinates> at $p$. Requiring the displayed inequality in every such chart therefore gives the <dominant energy condition> for every future <timelike vector>; positive rescaling handles nonunit vectors. Future <null vectors> follow by continuity if they are included in the definition. **The condition must hold in every local Lorentz frame; one chart alone is insufficient.**

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In an orthonormal frame, $F_{0i}=-E_i$ and $F_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. The <electromagnetic stress-energy tensor> gives
$$
T_{00}=\frac{E^2+B^2}{8\pi},\qquad T_{0i}=-\frac{(\mathbf E\times\mathbf B)_i}{4\pi}.
$$
The sign of $T_{0i}$ follows directly from $F_{0j}F_i{}^j=-E_j\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. Thus
$$
T_{00}^2-\sum_iT_{0i}^2
=\frac{(E^2-B^2)^2+4(\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B)^2}{64\pi^2}\ge0,
$$
and $T_{00}\ge0$. These formulas hold in every local <Lorentz frame>, so the criterion just proved establishes the <dominant energy condition>. Equality occurs for a nonzero null <Maxwell field> with $E^2=B^2$ and $\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0$, whose energy current is <null vector>.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the <P(X, phi) scalar field theory> specialized to $F(X)$, vary the inverse <metric tensor> while holding $\phi$ fixed. With $q_a=\partial_a\phi$,
$$
\delta X=-\frac12q_aq_b\delta g^{ab},\qquad \delta\sqrt{-g}=-\frac12\sqrt{-g}\,g_{ab}\delta g^{ab}.
$$
Using $T_{ab}=-2(-g)^{-1/2}\delta S/\delta g^{ab}$ yields
$$
\boxed{T_{ab}=F'(X)q_aq_b+F(X)g_{ab}.}
$$
In any local <Lorentz frame>, set $A=F'(X)\ge0$, $Y=XF'(X)-F(X)\ge0$, $s=\sum_iq_i^2$, and $X=(q_0^2-s)/2$. Then
$$
\rho=T_{00}=Aq_0^2-F=\frac A2(q_0^2+s)+Y\ge0,\qquad T_{0i}=Aq_0q_i.
$$
A direct calculation gives
$$
\boxed{\rho^2-\sum_iT_{0i}^2=A^2X^2+A(q_0^2+s)Y+Y^2\ge0.}
$$
Hence $\rho\ge\sqrt{T_{0i}T_{0i}}$ in every local <Lorentz frame>, proving the <dominant energy condition>. This proof covers timelike, null, spacelike, and zero $\nabla\phi$ alike; it does not assume $X>0$ or use a <perfect fluid in general relativity> rest frame. The hypotheses need only hold at every attained value of $X$; the stated all-$X$ assumptions are sufficient. For example, $F=X-V_0$, $V_0\ge0$, satisfies them.

